From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:32:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kk: report a discrete device type to Red Dead Redemption 2

KosmicKrisp reports an integrated GPU to every application. Red Dead
Redemption 2's Vulkan renderer, on an integrated GPU, creates its
images linear: about 11,500 in Story mode, among them 2D arrays, 3D
images and mip chains that Metal cannot make from a buffer. It writes
the mip chains and some of the 3D images through their mapping, at the
offsets vkGetImageSubresourceLayout reports, with no GPU write; the
texture KosmicKrisp gives such an image does not read what the mapping
holds. On a discrete GPU, with the same memory types and heap, the
renderer makes one linear image (2D, transfer only, which Metal makes
from a buffer) and uploads and reads back through buffer copies
instead, with about the same memory.

Report a discrete GPU to the instance whose application name is "Red
Dead Redemption 2" and whose engine name is "SGA", both exactly, and
log it. The executable name is not used: on iOS it is the app that
runs Wine. The game's process also makes DXVK and vkd3d-proton
instances (application RDR2.exe); those keep the integrated type, as
does every other application. KosmicKrisp has no driconf option table,
and this follows the engine-name check it already makes for Zink.
Nothing else in KosmicKrisp or the Vulkan runtime it uses depends on
the device type. MESA_KK_DEBUG=integrated_device reports the integrated
type to the game as well, to compare the two.

Mesa main (9a1e58da, checked on 2026-10-18) reports an integrated GPU
to every application.

Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-17-rdr2-vulkan-needs.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.c           |  1 +
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.h           |  3 ++
 src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++-
 3 files changed, 35 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.c b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.c
index ad0812ed..527c8ee0 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@ const struct debug_named_value debug_flags[] = {
    {"draws", KK_DEBUG_DRAWS},
    {"solid", KK_DEBUG_SOLID},
    {"os26_workarounds", KK_DEBUG_OS26_WORKAROUNDS},
+   {"integrated_device", KK_DEBUG_INTEGRATED_DEVICE},
    {NULL, 0},
 };
 
diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.h b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.h
index 3d11a1dc..2ddff857 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.h
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_debug.h
@@ -29,6 +29,9 @@ enum kk_debug {
     * miscompile or memory-model problem those workarounds cover from the
     * rest */
    KK_DEBUG_OS26_WORKAROUNDS = 1ull << 7,
+   /* Report the integrated device type to the applications that get the
+    * discrete one (kk_get_device_type), to compare */
+   KK_DEBUG_INTEGRATED_DEVICE = 1ull << 8,
 };
 
 extern enum kk_debug kk_mesa_debug_flags;
diff --git a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c
index 0efbd147..c8e36a03 100644
--- a/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/kosmickrisp/vulkan/kk_physical_device.c
@@ -555,6 +555,36 @@ kk_get_device_features(
    };
 }
 
+/* Red Dead Redemption 2's Vulkan renderer (application "Red Dead Redemption
+ * 2", engine "SGA") creates about 11,500 linear images on an integrated GPU,
+ * and writes its linear mip chains and some 3D images through their mapping;
+ * Metal cannot make those shapes from a buffer, and the texture KosmicKrisp
+ * gives them instead does not read what the mapping holds. On a discrete GPU
+ * it makes one linear image (2D, transfer only) and copies through buffers
+ * instead, in the same memory. Matched by the instance's application and
+ * engine names, exactly: on iOS the executable is the app that runs Wine,
+ * and the game's process makes DXVK and vkd3d-proton instances (application
+ * RDR2.exe), which keep the integrated type. MESA_KK_DEBUG=integrated_device
+ * reports integrated to it as well, to compare.
+ */
+static VkPhysicalDeviceType
+kk_get_device_type(const struct kk_instance *instance)
+{
+   const char *app = instance->vk.app_info.app_name;
+   const char *engine = instance->vk.app_info.engine_name;
+   if (!app || strcmp(app, "Red Dead Redemption 2") || !engine ||
+       strcmp(engine, "SGA"))
+      return VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_INTEGRATED_GPU;
+
+   if (KK_DEBUG(INTEGRATED_DEVICE)) {
+      fprintf(stderr, "[kk-device] %s (%s): integrated device type "
+              "(MESA_KK_DEBUG=integrated_device)\n", app, engine);
+      return VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_INTEGRATED_GPU;
+   }
+   fprintf(stderr, "[kk-device] %s (%s): discrete device type\n", app, engine);
+   return VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_DISCRETE_GPU;
+}
+
 static void
 kk_get_device_properties(
    const struct kk_physical_device *pdev, const struct kk_instance *instance,
@@ -585,7 +615,7 @@ kk_get_device_properties(
       .vendorID =
          instance->force_vk_vendor != 0 ? instance->force_vk_vendor : 0x106b,
       .deviceID = 100,
-      .deviceType = VK_PHYSICAL_DEVICE_TYPE_INTEGRATED_GPU,
+      .deviceType = kk_get_device_type(instance),
 
       /* Vulkan 1.0 limits */
       /* Values taken from Apple7
